{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The decision problem does not contain a sure outcome (i.e., no outcome has 100% probability), so the reference value is zero. The reason evaluates the mean difference between each outcome and the reference value (which is simply the outcome itself, since reference = 0), weighted by their probabilities. For Lottery A: outcomes are 4000 (25%), 2000 (25%), and 0 (50%). The mean difference is (4000 × 0.25) + (2000 × 0.25) + (0 × 0.50) = 1000 + 500 + 0 = 1500. For Lottery B: outcomes are 6000 (25%) and 0 (75%). The mean difference is (6000 × 0.25) + (0 × 0.75) = 1500. Thus, both lotteries have the same mean difference (1500), so the decision reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
